  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon water vapor satellite imagery along with upper \nair analysis showed a low amplitude upper level Trough across WY and \nCO. An upper level trough was lifting northeast across IL. An upper \nlevel ridge extended from the lower MS River Valley southwest into \nsouth TX. A more amplified upper level trough was located across the \neastern Pacific, west of CA.\n\nthe 18Z surface map showed a surface cold front extending southwest \nfrom New England across the OH River valley, then southwest across \nnortheast OK, extending farther southwest across the southern TX \nPNHDL into northeast NM. Temperatures at noon were in the mid to \nupper 60s north with mid to upper 70s along and south of I-70.\n\nToday through Tonight:\n\nThe H5 trough across WY into northern CO will shift east. The \nstronger ascent will be across NE ahead of the H5 trough axis, but \nthe southern extent of the ascent may cause a few showers across the \nnorthern counties of the CWA Tonight into early Monday morning. The \nsouthern counties may see a few showers this afternoon into the \nearly evening hours. The stronger isentropic upglide will be across \nsouthern KS but there maybe just enough ascent for a few rainshowers \nto move across areas along and southeast of I-35. Most CAMs do not\nshow much shower activity across the CWA. Late tonight a complex of\nstorms across southeast CO will move east but should weaken before\nreaching the southwest counties of the CWA by early Monday morning.\n\nSkies will be cloudy Today, so afternoon highs will not get much \nwarmer then current temperatures in the 60s north and 70s south.\n\nMonday through Wednesday night:\n\nTemperatures will remain cool with mid to upper 60s north and lower \nto mid 70s south. The southern stream H5 ridge will amplify across \neastern TX into OK through midweek. However the surface front will \npush south across central TX and then become stationary. I cannot \nrule out an isolated shower during this period, since a few mid-level\nperturbations may over the H5 ridge axis and provide enough ascent \nfor isolated showers. \n\nThursday through Sunday:\n\nThe extended range models are in good agreement Moving an H5 trough \nonshore across the Pacific northwest with a series of mid level \nperturbations lifting northeast across the Plains Thursday through \nSaturday. . A surface cold front will be slowly moving southeast \nacross the state of KS. Expect chances for showers and thunderstorms\nThursday through Saturday night. The highest PoPs may occur Thursday\nnight into Friday where a combination of isentropic lift and ascent \nahead of an H5 perturbation will lead to widespread showers, with a \nfew thunderstorms. Sunday morning the surface front will push \nsoutheast of the CWA, bringing an end to the rain chances. The front \nwill be more of a Pacific front, so it may be a few degrees warmer on\nSunday with more insolation. Highs Thursday and Friday will be in \nthe mid to upper 60s north to lower to mid 70s south. Highs Saturday \nand Sunday will be a bit warmer with mid 70s north and lower to mid \n80s south.",
